{
  "name": "The Best Way to Sand Drywall for a Flawless Finish",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Getting a truly flawless drywall finish really boils down to proper sanding, and there's a right way to do it. First off, and this is super important, make absolutely sure your joint compound is bone dry. If it's even a little damp, you'll just gum up your sandpaper and create more problems than you solve. Waiting at least overnight after your final mud coat is usually a good rule of thumb. When you start sanding, use light, even pressure. You're not trying to carve into the wall; you're just gently knocking down high spots and blending the edges of the mud. Start with a medium-grit sandpaper (around 120-150 grit) for initial smoothing, and then move to a finer grit (180-220 grit) for the final pass. Using a sanding sponge or a pole sander can help you maintain even pressure over larger areas. Always check your work with your hand, feeling for any bumps or ridges, and also use an angled light. This kind of lighting will highlight any imperfections that might be invisible in direct light. Finally, after all the sanding, make sure you thoroughly clean the wall of all dust before priming. A professional touch can make a huge difference, especially if you want that perfectly smooth, paint-ready surface without any headaches. Give us a call if you need a hand!",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
